The repository's \path{support/SOURCE-CATALOGUE.md} lists every supplied
extract, its byte-identical aliases, phrase matches, and the chapter
reading notes recorded for it. The catalogue is not the bibliography.
One operates on physical files; the other identifies publications and
editions. Keep both views when a claim depends on a particular passage.
For example, the title pages, not the filenames, identify the contents:
\path{asdl97.txt} duplicates the local text of Pierce's
\emph{Types and Programming Languages}~\cite{pierce2002types}.
An extract whose filename begins \texttt{methods-in-molecular-biology}
actually contains Michael Abrash's \emph{The Zen of Assembly Language}.
Ignoring the title page in the first case would invent a source for
abstract syntax description; in the second it would route a performance
engineering book to an unrelated subject.
The converse mistake also occurs: El-Metwally, Ouda, and Helmy's
\emph{Next Generation Sequencing Technologies and Challenges in Sequence
Assembly} is about a different meaning of \emph{assembly}, not about
mnemonics, relocation, or machine code. Treat it as a negative result
when searching for sources about assemblers.

A practical reading sequence is to start with the specification for a
normative claim, the original paper for an algorithm, and a textbook for
terminology and background. Next, identify the edition, section, and
conditions needed to reproduce the claim. For example, the near-matching
Intel optimization manual filenames need not describe the same revision.
Use the order number and revision printed in the manual, and cite the
target actually modeled in your experiment
(\cref{ch:ext-225,ch:ext-229}). Byte equality is enough to avoid treating
some files as independent witnesses. Byte \emph{inequality} is not enough
to show that two scans report different experiments.

The catalogue also provides phrase matches per distinct extract. This is a
retrieval device, not a measure of scholarly relevance: a work can match
\emph{register allocation} through a passing citation, and an OCR line
break inside a word can conceal a central discussion. Kruschwitz's
\emph{Intelligent Document Retrieval} contrasts the value of document
structure with undifferentiated text search; Marmanis and Babenko's
\emph{Algorithms of the Intelligent Web} distinguish retrieved candidates
from how the system ranks them. Apply the same distinction here: first use
matches to find candidate texts, then verify the passage in the paper or
standard. A textless extract can explain why there is no usable local
evidence, but it cannot substantiate the corresponding technical claim.

\subsection{A Cross-Disciplinary Reading Exercise}

Kernighan and Cherry's \emph{A System for Typesetting Mathematics} treats
Troff as a target for a small mathematical language. The front end must
recognize a fraction and its nesting; the target can place the parts using
the dimensions it knows. Van Wyk's \emph{A Graphics Typesetting Language}
instead lets an IDEAL box state constraints on named points before the
drawing is rendered. Try a fraction over a figure whose diameter changes:
the fraction markup fixes the grouping, while the figure language fixes
relative geometry. Lowering either to a sequence of independent marks
before solving its own layout problem would throw away needed information.
Bernstein's study of music printing in Renaissance Venice records the
practical difficulty of aligning separately printed notes and staves.
This is not evidence about compiler correctness; it makes concrete the
cost of moving an alignment obligation into a later stage. For the
language-design side of the example, see \cref{ch:ext-187}.

\subsection{Reading Several Compilers Against One Program}

Take a loop that calls a procedure while updating an array accumulator.
Trace its representation in Wirth's \emph{Compiler Construction} and
Brinch Hansen's \emph{On Pascal Compilers}. Look for the point at which a
lexical name becomes an access to a known frame position, and for where
the compiler or interpreter does the work of computing an array address.
Then draw the same loop as a control-flow graph using Aho and Ullman's
\emph{Principles of Compiler Design}, and compare that presentation to
the later \emph{Compilers: Principles, Techniques, and Tools} by Aho,
Lam, Sethi, and Ullman~\cite{aho2007compilers}. Cooper and Torczon's
\emph{Engineering a Compiler} gives another route from those blocks to
SSA: follow the accumulator through the loop back edge and the array
write through memory dependence rather than treating the array as just
another scalar name.

Appel's \emph{Modern Compiler Implementation} in its C, Java, and ML
editions offers three ways to organize the compiler itself. Compare the
representation of expression and statement nodes, not the syntax of the
implementation languages in isolation. The source program does not
change meaning just because one implementation uses variants and pattern
matching where another uses tagged structures or classes. On the
target side, \emph{Engineering a Compiler: VAX-11 Code Generation and
Optimization} documents how a real instruction set shapes the choice
among instruction sequences. Recompile the example twice, once allowing
an address calculation inside the load and once requiring a separate
instruction; mark exactly where the difference enters. This separates
changes in target policy from changes to control flow, observable array
effects, or the source calling convention
(\cref{ch:ext-182,ch:ext-205,ch:ext-225}).

\section{Key Texts in Programming Language Theory}\label{sec:biblio-plt}

\subsection{Foundational Texts}

\begin{description}
  \item[Pierce, \textit{Types and Programming Languages} (2002)]~\cite{pierce2002types} The standard introduction to type theory for computer scientists. Covers the untyped and simply-typed lambda calculi, type reconstruction, subtyping, polymorphism, and type systems for imperative and object-oriented languages. Exceptionally clear and well-organized.

  \item[Pierce, \textit{Advanced Topics in Types and Programming Languages} (2004)]~\cite{pierce2004advanced} A collection of chapters on advanced topics: system F, dependent types, intersection types, type refinement, and more. Each chapter is self-contained and written by an expert.

  \item[Harper, \textit{Practical Foundations for Programming Languages} (2016)]~\cite{harper2016practical} A comprehensive treatment of programming language theory from the perspective of type theory. Emphasizes the unity of all programming language features as manifestations of type structure. Dense but rewarding.
\end{description}

\subsection{Semantics}

\begin{description}
  \item[Winskel, \textit{The Formal Semantics of Programming Languages} (1993)] A classic introduction to operational, denotational, and axiomatic semantics. Clear, concise, and mathematically rigorous.

  \item[Gunter, \textit{Denotational Semantics} (1992)] A thorough treatment of domain theory and its application to programming language semantics. Covers complete partial orders, continuous functions, and recursive definitions.

  \item[Stirling, \textit{Modal and Temporal Logics for Processes} (2009)] Covers modal and temporal logics for reasoning about concurrent and reactive systems. Connects to process calculi and model checking.
\end{description}

\subsection{Lambda Calculus and Computation}

\begin{description}
  \item[Barendregt, \textit{The Lambda Calculus: Its Syntax and Semantics} (1984)] The definitive reference on the lambda calculus. Comprehensive coverage of reduction systems, type systems, and models.

  \item[Hindley and Seldin, \textit{Lambda-Calculus and Combinators: An Introduction} (2008)] An accessible introduction to the lambda calculus and combinatory logic. Covers both typed and untyped systems.
\end{description}

\section{Key Texts in Compiler Design}\label{sec:biblio-compilers}

\subsection{Comprehensive References}

\begin{description}
  \item[Muchnick, \textit{Advanced Compiler Design and Implementation} (1997)]~\cite{muchnick1997advanced} The definitive reference on modern compiler optimization. Covers intermediate representations, dataflow analysis, loop optimization, instruction selection, register allocation, and more. Encyclopedic in scope.

  \item[Aho, Lam, Sethi, Ullman, \textit{Compilers: Principles, Techniques, and Tools} (2007)]~\cite{aho2007compilers} The ``Dragon Book.'' The standard textbook for compiler courses. Covers lexical analysis, parsing, semantic analysis, intermediate representations, optimization, and code generation.

  \item[Appel, \textit{Modern Compiler Implementation in ML} (2004)]~\cite{appel2004modern} A practical compiler textbook using ML. Covers the full compiler pipeline with working code. Excellent for understanding how theory maps to implementation.
\end{description}

\subsection{Specialized Topics}

\begin{description}
  \item[Allen and Kennedy, \textit{Optimizing Compilers for Modern Architectures} (2002)] Focuses on loop optimization and parallelism. Covers the polyhedral model, dependence analysis, and automatic parallelization.

  \item[Cooper and Torczon, \textit{Engineering a Compiler} (2012)] A modern compiler textbook that emphasizes practical engineering concerns. Good coverage of SSA form and modern optimization techniques.

  \item[Grune, Vijayan, et al., \textit{Parsing Techniques} (2008)]~\cite{grune2012parsing} A comprehensive treatment of parsing algorithms, from LL and LR to GLR and GLL. The definitive reference on parsing.
\end{description}

\section{Key Texts in Type Theory}\label{sec:biblio-type-theory}

\begin{description}
  \item[L\"of, \textit{Intuitionistic Type Theory} (1984)] The foundational text on Martin-L\"of type theory. Defines the rules for dependent types, inductive types, and universes.

  \item[The HoTT Book, \textit{Homotopy Type Theory: Univalent Foundations of Mathematics} (2013)]~\cite{hottbook} Introduces homotopy type theory and the univalent foundations. A collaborative work by many researchers. Available freely online.

  \item[Norell, \textit{Towards a Practical Programming Language Based on Dependent Type Theory} (2007)] The PhD thesis behind Agda. Practical and well-written.

  \item[Abel, \"Ohman, and others, various] The Agda wiki and documentation provide extensive references on dependent type theory in practice.
\end{description}

\section{Key Texts in Semantics}\label{sec:biblio-semantics}

\subsection{Operational Semantics}

\begin{description}
  \item[Plotkin, ``A Structural Approach to Operational Semantics'' (1981, reprinted 2004)] The foundational paper on structural operational semantics (SOS). Introduces the format of inference rules for defining language semantics.

  \item[Milner, ``A Theory of Type Polymorphism in Programming'' (1978)] Introduces the Hindley--Milner type system and its inference algorithm. A landmark paper.
\end{description}

\subsection{Denotational Semantics}

\begin{description}
  \item[Scott, ``Outline of a Mathematical Theory of Computation'' (1970)] Scott's foundational paper on domain theory and denotational semantics. Introduces continuous lattices and the semantics of recursive definitions.

  \item[Stoy, \textit{Denotational Semantics: The Scott-Strachey Approach} (1977)] A clear introduction to denotational semantics using domain theory.
\end{description}

\subsection{Axiomatic Semantics}

\begin{description}
  \item[Hoare, ``An Axiomatic Basis for Computer Programming'' (1969)] The foundational paper on Hoare logic. Introduces the $\{P\}\;C\;\{Q\}$ notation for reasoning about imperative programs.

  \item[Apt and Older, \textit{Meaning of Imperative Programs} (2020)] A modern treatment of axiomatic semantics, covering Hoare logic, separation logic, and program verification.
\end{description}

\section{Key Texts in Program Analysis}\label{sec:biblio-analysis}

\begin{description}
  \item[Cousot and Cousot, ``Abstract Interpretation: A Unified Lattice Model for Static Analysis'' (1977)] The foundational paper on abstract interpretation. Introduces the Galois connection framework for relating concrete and abstract semantics.

  \item[Nielson, Nielson, and Hankin, \textit{Principles of Program Analysis} (1999)] A comprehensive textbook on program analysis. Covers dataflow analysis, abstract interpretation, and constraint-based analysis.

  \item[Jones and Muchnick, ``A Complex Approach to Flow Analysis'' (1981)] An early paper on combining dataflow analysis with abstract interpretation.

  \item[Myers, \textit{A Simple Theory of Abstract Interpretation} (lecture notes)] Clear lecture notes that introduce abstract interpretation from first principles.
\end{description}

\section{Key Texts in Category Theory for CS}\label{sec:biblio-category}

\begin{description}
  \item[Awodey, \textit{Category Theory} (2010)] A modern, accessible introduction to category theory. Suitable for computer scientists. Covers categories, functors, natural transformations, adjunctions, monads, and topoi.

  \item[Mac Lane, \textit{Categories for the Working Mathematician} (1998)] The standard reference for category theory. More advanced than Awodey; aimed at mathematicians but useful for computer scientists.

  \item[Milewski, \textit{Category Theory for Programmers} (2020)] A programmer-friendly introduction to category theory, with Haskell examples. Available freely online.

  \item[Spivak, \textit{Category Theory for the Sciences} (2014)] An introduction to category theory aimed at scientists and engineers. Emphasizes applications.
\end{description}

\subsection{Category Theory and Programming Languages}

\begin{description}
  \item[Goguen, ``What is Unification?'' (1989)] Connects category theory to unification and logic programming.

  \item[Wadler, ``The Essence of Functional Programming'' (1992)] Introduces monads for functional programming. A classic paper that brought monads to the attention of programmers.

  \item[Moggi, ``Notions of Computation and Monads'' (1991)] The foundational paper connecting monads to computational effects.
\end{description}

\section{Research Directions and Open Problems}\label{sec:open-problems}

\subsection{Active Research Areas}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Dependent types for programming}: Making dependent types practical for everyday programming. Languages like Idris 2, Lean 4, and F* are pushing this frontier.

  \item \textbf{Gradual typing}: Combining static and dynamic typing in a single language. The GradualTalk, TypeScript, and Pyret projects are active in this area.

  \item \textbf{Verified compilation}: Extending verification to larger, more practical compilers. The CertiCoq and CakeML projects are leading this effort.

  \item \textbf{Compiler optimization with ML}: Using machine learning to guide optimization decisions. The LLVM ML project and learned cost models are active areas.

  \item \textbf{Multi-level compilation}: The MLIR project is creating a unified framework for multi-level intermediate representations.

  \item \textbf{Quantum programming}: Developing languages, type systems, and compilers for quantum computers. Q\#, Quipper, and SILQ are active projects.

  \item \textbf{Effect systems}: Tracking and controlling computational effects in the type system. The Koka, Frank, and Eff projects are exploring this direction.

  \item \textbf{Session types for concurrency}: Using session types to ensure communication safety in concurrent programs. Active research in the semantics and implementation of session types.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Open Problems}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{The optimal compilation problem}: Can we find the ``best'' compilation of a program (in some formal sense)? This is related to the theory of optimal reduction in the lambda calculus.

  \item \textbf{Type inference for rich type systems}: Type inference is undecidable for System F and many extensions. Finding practical, decidable fragments is an ongoing challenge.

  \item \textbf{The verification gap}: How can we bridge the gap between formal specifications and practical implementations? Verified compilation helps, but the entire tool chain must be verified.

  \item \textbf{Scalable program synthesis}: Can we synthesize programs from specifications at scale? Current techniques work for small programs but do not scale to real-world software.

  \item \textbf{Energy-efficient compilation}: Can compilers optimize for energy consumption as well as performance? This requires new cost models and optimization strategies.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Summary}\label{sec:biblio-summary}

This bibliographic essay has surveyed the key texts and research directions in programming language theory, compiler design, type theory, semantics, program analysis, and category theory for computer science. The literature is vast and growing, but the core ideas are well-established and well-documented.

The reader who wishes to go deeper should:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Start with Pierce's \textit{TAPL}~\cite{pierce2002types} for type theory.
  \item Read Muchnick~\cite{muchnick1997advanced} for compiler optimization.
  \item Study Cousot and Cousot's papers for abstract interpretation.
  \item Explore Awodey~\cite{awodey2010} or Milewski for category theory.
  \item Follow the proceedings of POPL, ICFP, PLDI, LICS, and TAPL for current research.
\end{enumerate}

The field of programming language research is vibrant and productive. The ideas developed over the past six decades continue to evolve, and new connections are being discovered between programming languages and other areas of computer science and mathematics. The reader who masters the foundations presented in this book will be well-prepared to contribute to this ongoing endeavor.

\section{Key Texts in Program Analysis and Verification}\label{sec:biblio-analysis}

\subsection{Abstract Interpretation}

\begin{description}
  \item[Cousot and Cousot, ``Abstract Interpretation: A Unified Lattice Model for Static Analysis'' (1977)]~\cite{cousot1977abstract} The founding paper of abstract interpretation. Introduces the Galois connection framework for relating concrete and abstract semantics. Essential reading for anyone working in static analysis.

  \item[Cousot, ``Principles of Abstract Interpretation'' (2021)] A comprehensive monograph by the co-inventor of abstract interpretation. Covers the full theory: Galois connections, fixpoint semantics, abstract domains, widening, narrowing, and the design of precise abstract interpreters. Over 800 pages of detailed development.

  \item[Nielson, Nielson, and Hankin, \textit{Principles of Program Analysis} (1999)]~\cite{nielson1999principles} A textbook covering dataflow analysis, abstract interpretation, and program verification. Bridges the gap between theoretical foundations and practical analysis algorithms. Includes detailed treatments of control-flow analysis for functional languages.

  \item[Spallone, \textit{Abstract Interpretation of Higher-Order Programs}] Extends abstract interpretation to higher-order functional programs. Covers closure analysis, control-flow analysis, and the interplay between abstract interpretation and type systems.
\end{description}

\subsection{Model Checking}

\begin{description}
  \item[Clarke, Grumberg, and Peled, \textit{Model Checking} (1999)]~\cite{clarke1999model} The standard reference on model checking. Covers temporal logics (LTL, CTL, CTL*), symbolic model checking using BDDs, bounded model checking, and abstraction techniques.

  \item[Baier and Katoen, \textit{Principles of Model Checking} (2008)] A comprehensive modern textbook. Covers both linear-time and branching-time temporal logics, probabilistic model checking, and the relationship between model checking and automata theory.

  \item[Peled and Wolper, ``Partial Order Methods for Model Checking'' (1997)] Introduces partial order reduction, which combats the state explosion problem by exploiting the commutativity of independent transitions. A key technique for practical model checking of concurrent systems.
\end{description}

\subsection{Program Verification}

\begin{description}
  \item[Hoare, ``An Axiomatic Basis for Computer Programming'' (1969)]~\cite{hoare1969axiomatic} The founding paper of Hoare logic. Introduces the triple $\{P\}\;C\;\{Q\}$ for reasoning about imperative programs. Established the paradigm of axiomatic semantics.

  \item[Dijkstra, \textit{A Discipline of Programming} (1976)] Introduces the weakest-precondition calculus and the guarded-command language. A landmark in the development of program derivation and verification.

  \item[Morgan, \textit{Programming from Specifications} (1990)] Develops the refinement calculus, which provides a formal basis for stepwise program development from specifications. Bridges the gap between specification and implementation.

  \item[Chlipala, \textit{Certified Programming with Dependent Types} (2013)]~\cite{chlipala2013certified} A practical introduction to using Coq for building verified software. Covers dependent types, tactical proof engineering, and the construction of certified compilers and data structures.
\end{description}

\section{Key Texts in Semantics}\label{sec:biblio-semantics}

\subsection{Denotational Semantics}

\begin{description}
  \item[Scott, ``Outline of a Mathematical Theory of Computation'' (1970)]~\cite{scott1970outline} The founding paper of domain theory and denotational semantics. Scott's key insight was that recursive definitions can be understood as least fixed points in a domain of partial information.

  \item[Stoy, \textit{Denotational Semantics: The Scott--Strachey Approach} (1977)] The first textbook on denotational semantics. Presents the Scott--Strachey approach with clarity and precision. Covers environments, continuations, and the semantics of recursion.

  \item[Schmidt, \textit{Denotational Semantics} (1986)] A thorough treatment with emphasis on the mathematical foundations. Covers domain theory, continuous functions, and the construction of semantic functions for a variety of language features.

  \item[Gunter, \textit{Denotational Semantics} (1992)] An updated treatment that covers the modern perspective. Includes chapters on polymorphism, modules, and the relationship between denotational and operational semantics.
\end{description}

\subsection{Operational Semantics}

\begin{description}
  \item[Plotkin, ``A Structural Approach to Operational Semantics'' (1981)]~\cite{plotkin1981structural} The foundational paper on structural operational semantics (SOS). Introduces the style of transition systems that has become the standard for specifying programming language semantics.

  \item[Plotkin, ``The Semantics of Static and Dynamic Scoping'' (1975)] Compares the semantics of static and dynamic scoping using continuation-passing style. A classic paper that demonstrates the power of semantic techniques.

  \item[Wright and Felleisen, ``A Syntactic Approach to Type Soundness'' (1994)] Demonstrates the ``reduction semantics'' approach to proving type soundness. Introduces evaluation contexts as a technique for structuring operational semantics proofs. Widely used in modern semantics research.
\end{description}

\subsection{Axiomatic Semantics and Separation Logic}

\begin{description}
  \item[O'Hearn, ``Resources, Concurrency, and Local Reasoning'' (2007)] Introduces concurrent separation logic, which extends separation logic with reasoning about concurrent programs. The key insight is that local reasoning---reasoning about a thread's portion of the heap---scales to concurrent settings.

  \item[Reynolds, ``Separation Logic: A Logic for Shared Mutable Data Structures'' (2002)]~\cite{reynolds2002separation} The founding paper of separation logic. Introduces the separating conjunction $P * Q$ and the separating implication $P \wand Q$. Demonstrates how these connectives enable local reasoning about heap-manipulating programs.

  \item[Calcagno, O'Hearn, and Yang, ``Local Action and Abstract Separation Logic''] Extends separation logic to abstract resources and action-based reasoning. Connects to the theory of concurrent separation algebras.
\end{description}

\section{Key Texts in Category Theory for Computer Science}\label{sec:biblio-category}

\subsection{General References}

\begin{description}
  \item[Mac Lane, \textit{Categories for the Working Mathematician} (1998)]~\cite{maclane1998categories} The classic reference for category theory. Written for mathematicians but essential for anyone who wants to understand the full depth of categorical constructions. Dense but authoritative.

  \item[Awodey, \textit{Category Theory} (2010)]~\cite{awodey2010} A modern introduction that is accessible to computer scientists. Covers categories, functors, natural transformations, adjunctions, limits, colimits, and topos theory. Clear exposition with many examples.

  \item[Riehl, \textit{Category Theory in Context} (2016)] A concise but thorough introduction to category theory. Emphasizes the ``context'' in which categorical constructions are used: the 2-categorical perspective on universal properties.
\end{description}

\subsection{Category Theory for Programming}

\begin{description}
  \item[Milewski, \textit{Category Theory for Programmers}] An informal, code-oriented introduction to category theory. Available as a free online book. Covers categories, functors, natural transformations, monads, and adjunctions with Haskell examples.

  \item[Pierce, \textit{Basic Category Theory for Computer Scientists} (1991)]~\cite{pierce1991basic} A brief introduction aimed at computer scientists. Covers categories, functors, natural transformations, and universal properties with emphasis on applications to type theory and semantics.

  \item[Walters, \textit{Categories and Computer Science} (1992)] Covers categories, monoids, automata, and the connection between category theory and computation. Includes applications to data types and program semantics.

  \item[Spivak, \textit{Category Theory for the Sciences} (2014)] A broad introduction to category theory for scientists and engineers. Covers categories, functors, adjunctions, and applied category theory. Emphasizes the unifying power of categorical thinking.
\end{description}

\subsection{Topos Theory and Logic}

\begin{description}
  \item[Mac Lane and Moerdijk, \textit{Sheaves in Geometry and Logic} (1992)] An introduction to topos theory from the perspective of its logical applications. Shows how Grothendieck toposes generalize set theory and provide models for intuitionistic logic.

  \item[Goldblatt, \textit{Topoi: The Categorical Analysis of Idea} (2006)] A comprehensive introduction to topos theory and its logical significance. Covers the subobject classifier, the internal logic of a topos, and the connection to intuitionistic logic and Kripke semantics.
\end{description}

\section{Key Texts in Concurrency and Distributed Systems}\label{sec:biblio-concurrency}

\subsection{Process Calculi}

\begin{description}
  \item[Milner, \textit{A Calculus of Communicating Systems} (1980)]~\cite{milner1980ccs} The founding paper of CCS (Calculus of Communicating Systems). Introduces the key ideas of process algebra: parallel composition, communication, and bisimulation equivalence.

  \item[Milner, \textit{Communicating and Mobile Systems: The Pi-Calculus} (1999)] A textbook introduction to the pi-calculus. Covers the syntax, operational semantics, and type systems of the pi-calculus. Includes applications to mobility and security protocols.

  \item[Sangiorgi and Walker, \textit{The Pi-Calculus: A Theory of Mobile Processes} (2001)] A comprehensive treatment of the pi-calculus. Covers the full theory: bisimulation, type systems, expressiveness results, and applications to object-oriented programming and security.

  \item[Hoare, \textit{Communicating Sequential Processes} (1985)]~\cite{hoare1985csp} The classic text on CSP. Introduces the CSP process algebra and its application to concurrent programming. The theoretical foundation for the Go language's concurrency model.
\end{description}

\subsection{Type Systems for Concurrency}

\begin{description}
  \item[Honda and Vasconcelos, ``Language Primitives for Structured Communication'' (1998)] Introduces session types as a type system for communication protocols. Shows how session types ensure that concurrent processes communicate correctly.

  \item[Caires, Pfenning, and Toninho, ``Linear Logic as Resource Logic'' (2010)] Connects session types to linear logic, providing a logical foundation for communication-based programming. Shows how linearity ensures that communication resources are used exactly once.

  \item[Balzer, Pfenning, and Toninho, ``Linear Logical Relations for Session Types'' (2019)] Develops logical relations for session types, enabling proofs of observational equivalence and parametricity for concurrent programs.
\end{description}

\section{Key Texts in Dependently Typed Programming}\label{sec:biblio-dtp}

\begin{description}
  \item[Augustsson, ``Cayenne---a Language with Dependent Types'' (1998)] An early practical exploration of dependent types in a programming language. Demonstrates that dependent types can be integrated into a practical language design.

  \item[Brady, \textit{Idris: Systems Programming Meets Dependently Typed Programming} (2013)] Describes Idris, a general-purpose programming language with dependent types. Demonstrates that dependent types can be used for systems programming, not just theorem proving.

  \item[Brady, \textit{Type-Driven Development with Idris} (2017)]~\cite{brady2017type} A practical introduction to type-driven development using Idris. Shows how types can guide program construction, how to use dependent types for specification, and how to extract verified executables.

  \item[McBride and McKinna, ``The View from the Left'' (2004)] Introduces the ``view'' pattern for dependently typed programming. Demonstrates how dependent types can encode invariants that eliminate entire classes of runtime errors.

  \item[McBride, ``I Got Plenty o' Nuttin''' (2016)] Argues for the use of \emph{nothing} (the empty type) as a first-class concept in dependently typed programming. Shows how totality and the empty type enable more precise specifications.
\end{description}

\section{Key Texts in Language Implementation}\label{sec:biblio-implementation}

\subsection{Garbage Collection}

\begin{description}
  \item[Jones, Hosking, and Linnain, \textit{The Garbage Collection Handbook} (2012)]~\cite{jones2012gc} The definitive reference on garbage collection. Covers reference counting, mark-sweep, copying collectors, generational collectors, concurrent collectors, and distributed collectors. Essential for anyone implementing a language runtime.

  \item[Wilson, ``Unsyncronized Concurrent Collection'' (1992)] An early paper on concurrent garbage collection. Describes techniques for collecting garbage without stopping the mutator threads.

  \item[Blackburn et al., ``The Jikes Research Virtual Machine'' (2001)] Describes Jikes RVM, a research virtual machine for Java that supports experimentation with garbage collection algorithms. A key tool for GC research.
\end{description}

\subsection{Runtime Systems}

\begin{description}
  \item[Appel and McAllester, ``An Empirical Study of Type Transformations in a Standard ML Compiler'' (2001)] Studies the performance impact of different type representations in a compiler for Standard ML. Demonstrates the trade-offs between boxed and unboxed representations.

  \item[Peyton Jones, \textit{The Implementation of Functional Programming Languages} (1987)]~\cite{peytonjones1987implementation} A classic text on implementing functional languages. Covers lazy evaluation, graph reduction, the STG machine, and garbage collection for functional languages. Still relevant for understanding the implementation of Haskell and similar languages.

  \item[Marlow, \textit{Parallel and Concurrent Programming in Haskell} (2013)] Covers the runtime system of GHC, including the scheduler, garbage collector, and support for parallel and concurrent programming. Essential for understanding how modern functional language runtimes work.
\end{description}

\subsection{JIT Compilation}

\begin{description}
  \item[Ayers et al., ``Aggressive Inlining in the IBM Java JIT Compiler'' (2004)] Describes the inlining strategies used in a production JIT compiler. Shows how aggressive inlining enables further optimizations and improves performance.

  \item[Gal et al., ``A Comprehensive Approach to the Design of a Trace-Based JIT Compiler'' (2009)] Describes the design of a trace-based JIT compiler for JavaScript. Covers trace selection, trace tree construction, and on-stack replacement. The basis for many modern JavaScript JIT compilers.

  \item[Grimmer et al., ``A Language-Agnostic Model for JIT Compilation'' (2015)] Describes the Truffle framework, which provides a language-agnostic infrastructure for building JIT compilers. Demonstrates the Futamura projections in practice: a partial evaluator for interpreters yields compiled code.
\end{description}

