\subsection{Reading Strategies}

For the aspiring researcher, we recommend the following approach to reading the literature:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Start with surveys and tutorials.} Many conferences now include tutorial sessions. The ``Foundations and Trends'' series provides excellent surveys.
  \item \textbf{Read the classics.} Every subfield has a few foundational papers that everyone knows. Read these first: Scott (1970) for semantics, Cousot and Cousot (1977) for abstract interpretation, Milner (1978) for type inference.
  \item \textbf{Follow a research group.} Pick a group whose work interests you and read their recent papers. This gives you context and helps you understand the research landscape.
  \item \textbf{Read papers critically.} For each paper, ask: What problem is being solved? What is the key insight? What are the limitations? How does this relate to other work?
  \item \textbf{Reproduce results.} The best way to understand a paper is to implement its key ideas. Try to reproduce the examples, extend them, and see where the approach breaks down.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Extended Bibliographic Essay on Verified Compilation}\label{sec:biblio-verified}

Verified compilation is one of the most important applications of programming language theory. The goal is to prove that a compiler preserves the semantics of the source program: every behavior of the compiled code corresponds to a behavior of the source program.

\subsection{The CompCert Project}

\begin{description}
  \item[Leroy, ``Formal Verification of a Realistic Compiler'' (2009)]~\cite{leroy2009compcert} The founding paper of CompCert. Describes the formal verification of a realistic C compiler using Coq. The compiler handles most of C (excluding some preprocessor features) and generates PowerPC/ARM/x86 assembly. The verification proves that the compiled code behaves exactly as specified by the C semantics.

  \item[Leroy et al., ``The CompCert Memory Model'' (2012)] Describes the formal memory model used by CompCert. This model handles the subtleties of C's memory semantics: unaligned accesses, bit-fields, unions, and the distinction between defined and undefined behavior.

  \item[Blazy and Leroy, ``Mechanized Semantics for C'' (2009)] Describes the formal semantics of C that underlies CompCert. Covers the full language: expressions, statements, functions, types, and the memory model.
\end{description}

\subsection{Other Verified Compilers}

\begin{description}
  \item[Klein et al., ``seL4: The World's First Mathematically Proven Operating System Kernel'' (2009)] Describes the verification of the seL4 microkernel. While not a compiler, the verification techniques are closely related: a formal specification is refined down to C code, and the refinement is proved in Isabelle/HOL.

  \item[Malecha et al., ``Towards a Verified Relational Database Management System'' (2016)] Describes the verification of a relational database management system. Demonstrates that verification techniques can scale to complex systems software.

  \item[Owens et al., ``Verified Peephole Optimizations for a Realistic Compiler'' (2020)] Describes the verification of peephole optimizations for an ARM compiler. Shows how to verify individual optimization passes and compose their correctness proofs.

  \item[Parikh, ``The Verified Smart Contract Compiler'' (2020)] Describes the verification of a compiler for Ethereum smart contracts. Demonstrates the application of verified compilation to blockchain technology.
\end{description}

\subsection{Translation Validation}

An alternative to verified compilation is \emph{translation validation}: instead of proving the compiler correct once and for all, we validate each individual compilation. The validator checks that the output program is semantically equivalent to the input program.

\begin{description}
  \item[Pnueli, Siegel, and Singerman, ``Translation Validation'' (1998)] Introduces the idea of translation validation. Instead of verifying the compiler, we verify each translation by comparing the source and target programs.

  \item[Necula, ``Translation Validation for an Optimized C Compiler'' (2000)] Describes a translation validator for a realistic C compiler. Uses proof-carrying code techniques: the compiler generates a proof of equivalence that the validator checks.

  \item[Copperthwaite et al., ``RockSolid: A Verified Compiler for a Secure Operating System'' (2020)] Combines verified compilation with translation validation. The compiler is partially verified, and the remaining correctness conditions are checked by a validator for each compilation.
\end{description}

\section{Key Texts in Quantum and Probabilistic Programming}\label{sec:biblio-quantum}

\subsection{Quantum Programming Languages}

\begin{description}
  \item[Selinger, ``A Survey of Quantum Programming Languages'' (2004)] An early survey of quantum programming languages, covering QCL, QGCL, and quantum lambda calculi. Provides a taxonomy of quantum language designs.

  \item[Paykin, Rand, and Zdancewic, ``QWIRE: A Quantum Circuit Embedding in Coq'' (2017)] Describes QWIRE, a framework for reasoning about quantum circuits in Coq. Demonstrates how dependent types can be used to verify quantum programs.

  \item[Ying, \textit{Foundations of Quantum Programming} (2016)] A comprehensive textbook on quantum programming. Covers quantum lambda calculi, quantum Hoare logic, and model checking of quantum programs.
\end{description}

\subsection{Probabilistic Programming}

\begin{description}
  \item[Gordon et al., ``Probabilistic Programming'' (2014)] A survey of probabilistic programming languages and their applications. Covers Bayesian inference, approximate inference algorithms, and the design of probabilistic programming languages.

  \item[Staton, ``Commuting Conversions and the Semantics of Probabilistic Programming'' (2017)] Provides a categorical semantics for probabilistic programming languages using commutative monads and Markov categories.

  \item[Borgstr\"om et al., ``Denotational Validation of Higher-Order Bayesian Inference'' (2019)] Develops a denotational semantics for higher-order probabilistic programs and validates Bayesian inference algorithms against it.
\end{description}

\section{Online Resources and Communities}\label{sec:biblio-online}

The following online resources are invaluable for staying current with programming language research:

\begin{description}
  \item[The Programming Languages subreddit (\texttt{r/programming\_languages})] An active community for discussing language design, implementation, and theory.
  \item[The Lambda the Ultimate weblog] A long-running blog (now mostly archived) with extensive discussions of programming language research.
  \item[The Homotopy Type Theory blog] Covers developments in HoTT, cubical type theory, and univalent foundations.
  \item[The Coq Club mailing list] For discussing Coq development, proof engineering, and the Coq ecosystem.
  \item[The Lean Zulip chat] An active chat community for Lean users, from beginners to core developers.
  \item[PL People (\texttt{pl-people.org})] A database of programming language researchers and their publications.
  \item[The Verified Software mailing list] For discussing verification tools, including proof assistants, model checkers, and program verifiers.
\end{description}

\begin{insight}
The programming language community is unusually open and collaborative. Most researchers share preprints on arXiv, code on GitHub, and formalizations in public repositories. The barrier to entry for participating in research is lower than in many other areas of computer science: a good idea, a clear presentation, and a working prototype are often sufficient to make a contribution.
\end{insight}
